  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 205 AM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nSynoptically, from Wednesday through next Monday, upper level flow \nremains from the northwest as the ridge over the Four Corners region \nstays nearly stationary. Beginning around Friday into Saturday, the \nridge looks to weaken and shift east, transitioning upper level flow \nto come from the west/southwest by early Sunday morning. Guidance \nindicates a few shortwaves could move through the County Warning \nArea (CWA) over the weekend. \n\nHigh temperatures across the Tri-State area should remain in the 70s \nand 80s through Sunday. Saturday is currently forecast to be the \nwarmest day, with highs in the low to mid 80s across the majority of \nthe area. Overnight lows are forecast to stay in the 50s and 60s. \nProbabilities of Precipitation (PoPs) remain below 10% through \nSunday, though shortwaves moving through the area could increase \nchances if the shortwaves strengthen. \n\nWinds early in the period should be primarily be southerly at 5-15 \nmph with gusts to 20-25 mph. From Friday and Saturday through \nSunday, breezier afternoons are possible with gusts reaching 30-40 \nmph. Relative humidity (RH) values are forecast to stay above 20% \nfor most of the CWA, keeping fire weather concerns low. However, \nconditions in the Colorado Counties may require monitoring on \nSaturday, as RH values approach 20% alongside gusts of 25-35 mph. \nSome guidance suggests a front moving through the northwestern \nportions of the CWA, which could lower RH values further and \nincrease fire weather potential. \n\nLong range models begin to diverge around Sunday morning. The \nCanadian-NH, ECMWF-HiRes, and GFS all show an upper level trough, \naround 550 dam, originating from the Pacific-Northwest and setting \nup over the rockies near Idaho. As this trough moves through the \nregion, the early part of next week could see the best precipitation \nchances. However, if the trough progresses slower precipitation \nchances will shift later into next week.",
